  "image_name": "4d016a6e7647e4cc614b84e17cdce234.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic overview of a synovial joint and the cellular organization of the synovium. On the left, a whole-joint cross section shows the basic anatomy around the articulating bones, including cartilage, synovium, capsule, ligament, tendon, and nearby muscle. The enlarged view on the right focuses on the synovial tissue and divides it into a thin lining and a deeper sub-lining.\n\nThe lining appears as a specialized surface layer adjacent to cartilage, containing lining fibroblasts and lining-layer macrophages arranged in a compact barrier-like structure. Beneath this, the sub-lining is shown as a looser connective tissue with blood vessels and a wider mix of cells. Several fibroblast populations are distinguished by color and shape, suggesting different roles such as immune function, inflammation/remodeling, and cycling sub-lining fibroblasts. Immune cells, including T cells and B cells, are scattered in this deeper region, and pericytes sit around blood vessels.\n\nAt the lower edge, the synovium lies next to bone, where osteoblasts and osteoclasts are depicted, linking synovial activity to bone remodeling. Overall, the image emphasizes that the synovium is not uniform but contains spatially organized cell types with distinct functions in joint homeostasis and inflammation.",
